Files: vibrantix-backend/src/modules/compliance-frameworks/project-compliance-lookup.controller.js — server-side filter logic (~lines 392–406)

Root Cause: The filters assetType=no_assets (controls where hasAssets=false) and status=covered (controls where coverageStatus='covered') are applied independently as boolean AND conditions. A control can only be covered if it has assets contributing to its score. However, between PCL sync runs, a control's assets may have been removed while the snapshot still records it as covered. The result is a non-empty, confusing result set that contradicts the filter semantics.

Claude Fix Prompt: In project-compliance-lookup.controller.js filter logic, add a guard before applying filters:
```js
if (assetType === 'no_assets' && statusFilter === 'covered') {
  // Logically contradictory — covered controls always have assets in a valid PCL snapshot
  return res.json({ success: true, data: { ...baseResponse, controlsByDomain: [], totalFiltered: 0 } });
}
```
Additionally, add a frontend guard in FrameworkComplianceMap.jsx: when assetFilter === 'no_assets' is set, disable the status=covered option in the status Select (show it greyed with tooltip "Covered controls always have assets").

Files: vibrantix-backend/src/modules/compliance-frameworks/client-frameworks.controller.js — addToProject() — find-then-create pattern

Root Cause: addToProject() first calls ClientFramework.findOne({ organizationId, projectId, frameworkId }) to check for an existing record, then conditionally creates or updates. Between the findOne and the save/update, a second concurrent request can also read null (no existing doc) and both proceed to ClientFramework.create(). The compound unique index (organizationId, projectId, frameworkId) will reject the second insert with a MongoServerError E11000, which surfaces as an unhandled 500 error.

Claude Fix Prompt: Replace the find-then-create pattern in addToProject() with an atomic findOneAndUpdate with upsert:
```js
const result = await ClientFramework.findOneAndUpdate(
  { organizationId, projectId, frameworkId },
  { $set: { status: 'active', addedBy: req.userId, addedAt: new Date() } },
  { upsert: true, new: true, runValidators: true }
);
```
This is atomic at the MongoDB level and naturally handles both "create new" and "reactivate archived" cases without a race condition.

Files: vibrantix-client-panel/src/components/grc/FrameworkComplianceMap.jsx — exportCSV() function, lines 99–101

Root Cause: The CSV serialisation escapes internal double-quotes (replace(/"/g, '""')) and wraps all values in double-quotes, which is correct RFC 4180 encoding. However, it does not sanitise against CSV formula injection (also called CSV injection or formula injection): if a control title, domain, or evidence/policy title begins with =, +, -, or @, Microsoft Excel, LibreOffice Calc, and Google Sheets will interpret the value as a formula when the file is opened. A malicious control name in the Vibrantix catalog (or an evidence title entered by a malicious actor) such as =CMD|' /C calc'!A0 could trigger code execution on the auditor's machine when they open the exported CSV. This is a supply-chain attack vector on compliance officers who routinely export and share these files.

Claude Fix Prompt: In exportCSV(), add a sanitisation step applied to every user-data string cell value before quoting:
```js
function csvSafe(val) {
  const s = String(val ?? '');
  // Prefix with single-quote if value starts with formula trigger characters
  if (['=', '+', '-', '@', '\t', '\r'].includes(s[0])) return "'" + s;
  return s;
}
```
Update the CSV serialisation line to:
```js
const csv = rows
  .map(r => r.map(v => `"${csvSafe(v).replace(/"/g, '""')}"`).join(','))
  .join('\n');
```
This is the OWASP-recommended mitigation for CSV injection. Apply csvSafe to all user-sourced string cells: ctrl.title, ctrl.domain, e.title, p.title, v.vendorName, ctrl.controlStringId.
